<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pakubuwono III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1788">1788</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pakubuwono III was susuhunan of Surakarta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> from 1749 to 1788, raised to the throne under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> protection during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-javanese-war-of-succession">Third Javanese War of Succession</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 1755 divided the Mataram realm, leaving him the eastern half while Mangkubumi became sultan of Yogyakarta, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a> of 1757 created Mangkunegaran from further lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-iii.jpg" alt="Pakubuwono III" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pakubuwana_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born at Kartasura Palace on 25 February 1732, the fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwana II</a> by his queen, Kanjeng Ratu Mas, a daughter of Pangeran Adipati Purbaya; his parents were first cousins. Born Raden Mas Gusti Suryadi, he was later named Raden Mas Gusti Suryakusuma and then, as crown prince, Kanjeng Gusti Pangeran Adipati Anom. While crown prince he married Raden Ajeng Wuku, a daughter of Adipati Cakraningrat, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, who was later dismissed and sent home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He took the throne on 15 December 1749 while his father lay critically ill. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwana II</a>'s disputed deathbed mandate had given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> the right to settle the succession, and the Company's governor, Baron von Hohendorff, installed him. He inherited a kingdom divided by the rebellion of his uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Prince Mangkubumi</a>, which had begun in 1746; three days before his accession the rebels had proclaimed Mangkubumi as a rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i">Pangeran Sambernyawa</a> as his patih.</p><p>By 1752 the two rebel leaders had fallen out, and the Company offered Mangkubumi a separate peace. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 13 February 1755 Mangkubumi received half the realm but was barred from the title of susuhunan, and ruled as Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Hamengkubuwana I</a> of Yogyakarta. Sambernyawa, feeling betrayed, fought on against both rulers until he opened talks in 1756; by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a> in March 1757 he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i">Mangkunegara I</a>, holding lands granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His political weakness left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> tense, with factions seeking to control his government. He was skilled in music, played the bonang agêng at bedhaya performances, and raised a dancer, Mas Rara Bêruk, to be his queen. He died on 26 September 1788 and was succeeded by their son, Pakubuwana IV, regarded as a more capable and assertive ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pakubuwono III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Giyanti in 1755 divided the realm, leaving <b>Pakubuwono III</b> with the eastern half while the Yogyakarta Sultanate took the west, and in 1757 further lands went to Mangkunegaran.</blockquote><a href="/state/surakarta-sunanate">Surakarta Sunanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Surakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Surakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.57,110.82&amp;year=1768" aria-label="Surakarta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pakubuwono III"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4225 5645h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4225 5645h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.57,110.82&amp;year=1768">What was here in 1768? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
